# Compiling a publication PDF

This chapter owns `src/publication-pdf.ts`. Pandoc conversion and destination
protection belong to the caller. Here a trusted publication plan becomes a PDF
in a private temporary directory. TeX and Lua can read files and execute code:
turning off shell escape is defense in depth, **not a sandbox for untrusted TeX**.
No tools are downloaded, no user destination is opened, and no shell is spawned
by this adapter. latexmk itself invokes its configured TeX engine.


The public compiler has no process-specific options. A factory supplies the test
seam without changing the CLI-facing signature; injected runners are trusted and
must honor their request's timeout, output bound, and completion contract.

```ts : <<publication PDF imports>>=
import { spawn, ChildProcess } from 'node:child_process';
import { constants } from 'node:fs';
import * as fs from 'node:fs/promises';
import * as path from 'node:path';
import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';
```

Failures retain the same diagnostic information as success. A retained directory
is caller-owned, including after failure; otherwise cleanup runs in `finally`.
Validation failures occurring before staging have no build directory to retain.

```ts : <<publication PDF API>>=
export interface PdfCompileOptions { keepBuild?: boolean; checkCancelled?: () => void; }
export interface PdfCompilation {
    pdf: Uint8Array;
    warnings: string[];
    buildDirectory?: string;
}
export type PdfCompiler = (files: readonly { path: string; content: string | Uint8Array }[],
    entry: string, options?: PdfCompileOptions) => Promise<PdfCompilation>;
export class PdfCompilationError extends Error {
    constructor(message: string, public readonly buildDirectory?: string,
        public readonly warnings: string[] = []) {
        super(message);
        this.name = 'PdfCompilationError';
    }
}
```

The normal runner bounds aggregate stdout and stderr, not each stream separately.
Its promise settles only after draining the pipes, or explicitly closing pipes
which a stray descendant kept open. Each invocation has its own process group
on POSIX. The default runner explicitly rejects Windows before spawning anything:
without process-tree-safe termination, direct-child termination is insufficient.
Injected runners can still exercise the compiler independently on Windows.

```ts : <<publication PDF process types>>=
export interface PdfProcessRequest {
    command: string;
    args: readonly string[];
    cwd: string;
    env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv;
    timeoutMs: number;
    maximumOutputBytes: number;
}
export interface PdfProcessResult { code: number | null; output: string; }
export type PdfProcessRunner = (request: PdfProcessRequest) => Promise<PdfProcessResult>;
const maximumLogBytes = 4 * 1024 * 1024;
const maximumPdfBytes = 64 * 1024 * 1024;
const buildTimeoutMs = 480_000;
```

Group termination also happens on a normal leader exit: a successful latexmk
must not leave descendants behind. An exit hook handles synchronous parent exit;
SIGINT, SIGTERM and SIGHUP cancel the invocation with an actionable error. The
host can handle that rejected promise using its normal command failure policy.

```ts : <<publication PDF process termination>>=
function killProcessTree(child: ChildProcess): void {
    if (!child.pid) { return; }
    try {
        process.kill(-child.pid, 'SIGKILL');
    } catch (error) {
        if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code !== 'ESRCH') {
            try { child.kill('SIGKILL'); } catch { /* The process may already be gone. */ }
        }
    }
}
interface ProcessCapture {
    chunks: Buffer[]; bytes: number; failure?: Error;
    drainTimer?: NodeJS.Timeout;
}
```

Spawn always uses an argument vector and ignored stdin. Timeouts cover the entire
invocation, including pipe closure. The short drain grace period is only a
fallback for inherited descriptors; ordinary output is read until `close`.

```ts : <<publication PDF process execution>>=
export const runPdfProcess: PdfProcessRunner = request => new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    if (process.platform === 'win32') {
        reject(new Error('PDF compilation is unsupported on Windows: process-tree-safe termination is unavailable'));
        return;
    }
    const child = spawn(request.command, [...request.args], {
        cwd: request.cwd, env: request.env, shell: false,
        detached: true, stdio: ['ignore', 'pipe', 'pipe']
    });
    const capture: ProcessCapture = { chunks: [], bytes: 0 };
    const stop = (error?: Error) => {
        capture.failure ??= error;
        killProcessTree(child);
        capture.drainTimer ??= setTimeout(() => {
            child.stdout?.destroy(); child.stderr?.destroy();
        }, 1000);
    };
    const timer = setTimeout(() => stop(new Error(
        `${request.command} timed out after ${request.timeoutMs} ms`)), request.timeoutMs);
    const cleanup = processHooks(child, stop);
    captureProcess(child, request, capture, stop);
    finishProcess(child, capture, timer, cleanup, resolve, reject);
});
```

Once the bound is exceeded, subsequent data is discarded while both streams
continue draining. We never concatenate an unbounded transcript. Stream errors
are failures too, rather than unhandled events or silent partial diagnostics.

```ts : <<publication PDF process capture>>=
function captureProcess(child: ChildProcess, request: PdfProcessRequest,
    capture: ProcessCapture, stop: (error?: Error) => void): void {
    const data = (chunk: Buffer) => {
        const remaining = Math.max(0, request.maximumOutputBytes - capture.bytes);
        if (remaining) { capture.chunks.push(chunk.subarray(0, remaining)); }
        capture.bytes += chunk.length;
        if (capture.bytes > request.maximumOutputBytes) {
            stop(new Error(`${request.command} exceeded its output limit`));
        }
    };
    for (const stream of [child.stdout, child.stderr]) {
        stream?.on('data', data);
        stream?.on('error', stop);
    }
    child.once('error', error => stop(error));
    child.once('exit', () => stop());
}
```

Hooks are removed on every completion, including spawn failure. The runner does
not retain global build state, so independent compiler invocations can overlap.

```ts : <<publication PDF process lifecycle>>=
function processHooks(child: ChildProcess, stop: (error?: Error) => void): () => void {
    const exit = () => killProcessTree(child);
    const signals = ['SIGINT', 'SIGTERM', 'SIGHUP'] as const;
    const handlers = signals.map(signal => () => stop(new Error(`PDF compilation interrupted by ${signal}`)));
    process.on('exit', exit);
    signals.forEach((signal, index) => process.on(signal, handlers[index]));
    return () => {
        process.removeListener('exit', exit);
        signals.forEach((signal, index) => process.removeListener(signal, handlers[index]));
    };
}
function finishProcess(child: ChildProcess, capture: ProcessCapture, timer: NodeJS.Timeout,
    cleanup: () => void, resolve: (result: PdfProcessResult) => void, reject: (error: Error) => void): void {
    child.once('close', (code, signal) => {
        clearTimeout(timer); clearTimeout(capture.drainTimer); cleanup();
        const output = Buffer.concat(capture.chunks).toString('utf8');
        const failure = capture.failure ?? (signal ? new Error(`Process terminated by ${signal}`) : undefined);
        if (failure) { reject(new Error(`${failure.message}\n${output}`.trim())); }
        else { resolve({ code, output }); }
    });
}
```

Compiler signal handlers span staging, preflight, log/PDF reads, and cleanup,
not just subprocess execution. The first signal records cancellation; subsequent
signals leave cleanup running. Filesystem operations already in flight finish
before their next checkpoint throws. Runner hooks independently kill live process
groups. Diagnostic reads and cleanup deliberately remain available after cancellation.

```ts : <<publication PDF compiler lifetime>>=
function compilerLifetime(): { check: () => void; dispose: () => void } {
    let cancellation: Error | undefined;
    const signals = ['SIGINT', 'SIGTERM', 'SIGHUP'] as const;
    const handlers = signals.map(signal => () => {
        cancellation ??= new Error(`PDF compilation interrupted by ${signal}`);
    });
    signals.forEach((signal, index) => process.on(signal, handlers[index]));
    return {
        check: () => { if (cancellation) { throw cancellation; } },
        dispose: () => signals.forEach((signal, index) => process.removeListener(signal, handlers[index]))
    };
}
function checkedRunner(runner: PdfProcessRunner, check: () => void): PdfProcessRunner {
    return async request => {
        check();
        const result = await runner(request);
        check();
        return result;
    };
}
```

Plan names are portable, normalized relative paths, never traversal instructions.
The staged entry has the fixed name `book.tex` in its original relative directory.
Companions remain in their planned locations, so relative TeX paths still work.
Case-folded collisions are rejected even on case-sensitive hosts. The working
directory's `book.*` namespace is reserved for compiler output, not supplied data.

```ts : <<publication PDF plan validation>>=
function safePlanPath(value: string): string {
    if (!value || /[\\:\u0000-\u001f\u007f]/u.test(value) || value.startsWith('/') ||
        value.split('/').some(part => !part || part === '.' || part === '..' || /[. ]$/.test(part) ||
            /^(con|prn|aux|nul|com[1-9]|lpt[1-9])(?:\.|$)/i.test(part))) {
        throw new Error(`Unsafe publication path: ${JSON.stringify(value)}`);
    }
    return value;
}
function prepareFiles(files: Parameters<PdfCompiler>[0], entry: string): Parameters<PdfCompiler>[0] {
    safePlanPath(entry);
    if (!entry.endsWith('.tex')) { throw new Error('PDF entry must be a .tex path'); }
    const fixed = path.posix.join(path.posix.dirname(entry), 'book.tex');
    const seen = new Set<string>();
    const prepared = files.map(file => {
        const name = safePlanPath(file.path);
        const staged = name === entry ? fixed : name;
        if (seen.has(staged.toLowerCase()) || (name !== entry &&
            path.posix.dirname(name).toLowerCase() === path.posix.dirname(entry).toLowerCase() &&
            /^book\./i.test(path.posix.basename(name)))) { throw new Error(`Conflicting publication path: ${name}`); }
        seen.add(staged.toLowerCase());
        return { path: staged, content: file.content };
    });
    if (!files.some(file => file.path === entry)) { throw new Error(`Missing PDF entry: ${entry}`); }
    return prepared;
}
```

Only fresh regular files are written, with exclusive creation and restrictive
permissions. Parent/file conflicts fail during staging and follow normal cleanup.
The input buffers are not modified. mkdtemp provides an unpredictable private root.

```ts : <<publication PDF staging>>=
async function stageFiles(directory: string, files: Parameters<PdfCompiler>[0], check: () => void): Promise<void> {
    check();
    await fs.chmod(directory, 0o700);
    for (const file of files) {
        check();
        const destination = path.join(directory, ...file.path.split('/'));
        await fs.mkdir(path.dirname(destination), { recursive: true, mode: 0o700 });
        check();
        await fs.writeFile(destination, file.content, { flag: 'wx', mode: 0o600 });
    }
    check();
}
async function buildEnvironment(directory: string): Promise<NodeJS.ProcessEnv> {
    const cache = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(directory, '.tex-cache-'));
    return { ...process.env, TEXMFOUTPUT: directory, TEXMFVAR: cache, TEXMFCACHE: cache,
        openout_any: 'p', shell_escape: 'f' };
}
```

User TeX installations and search configuration, including TEXMFHOME, TEXMFCONFIG
and TEXINPUTS, remain available. TEXMFOUTPUT cannot redirect output into a caller's
directory; writable TeX/font caches are private. SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH is preserved
when supplied, not invented: byte-identical PDF output is not promised.

```ts : <<publication PDF environment>>=
function requestFor(command: string, args: readonly string[], cwd: string,
    env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv, timeoutMs = 15_000): PdfProcessRequest {
    return { command, args, cwd, env, timeoutMs, maximumOutputBytes: maximumLogBytes };
}
const requiredTexFiles = [
    'scrbook.cls', 'fontspec.sty', 'geometry.sty', 'fvextra.sty', 'needspace.sty',
    'xcolor.sty', 'microtype.sty', 'amssymb.sty', 'amsmath.sty', 'graphicx.sty',
    'ulem.sty', 'hyperref.sty'
];
```

Preflight reflects the publication LaTeX template, including its KOMA book class.
kpsewhich uses the LuaLaTeX program name to honor engine-specific configuration.
Missing executables and packages give installation guidance without invoking a
package manager. Even the latexmk version probe uses `-norc`: version queries can
otherwise execute rc files. Font availability remains LuaLaTeX/fontspec's responsibility.

```ts : <<publication PDF dependencies>>=
async function preflight(runner: PdfProcessRunner, cwd: string, env: NodeJS.ProcessEnv): Promise<void> {
    for (const command of ['latexmk', 'lualatex', 'kpsewhich']) {
        try {
            const args = command === 'latexmk' ? ['-norc', '--version'] : ['--version'];
            const result = await runner(requestFor(command, args, cwd, env));
            if (result.code !== 0) { throw new Error(result.output || `exit ${result.code}`); }
        } catch (error) {
            throw new Error(`Cannot run ${command}. Install a TeX distribution with latexmk and LuaLaTeX, ` +
                `and ensure ${command} is on PATH. ${errorMessage(error)}`);
        }
    }
    const missing: string[] = [];
    for (const file of requiredTexFiles) {
        const result = await runner(requestFor('kpsewhich', ['-progname=lualatex', file], cwd, env));
        if (result.code !== 0 || !result.output.trim()) { missing.push(file); }
    }
    if (missing.length) { throw new Error(`Missing TeX packages: ${missing.join(', ')}. ` +
        'Install these using your TeX distribution package manager, then retry.'); }
}
```

Read descriptors, not unchecked paths. The nonblocking open prevents a FIFO from
hanging before the regular-file check; no-follow plus lstat protects against links
on supported platforms. A size-plus-one read detects growth beyond the measured
size. PDF acceptance requires a header and a bounded, nonempty regular file, not
merely a successful process exit; this is not a full PDF parser.

```ts : <<publication PDF bounded files>>=
async function readBoundedFile(filename: string, limit: number, check: () => void = () => {}): Promise<Buffer> {
    check(); const before = await fs.lstat(filename);
    check();
    if (!before.isFile() || before.isSymbolicLink() || before.nlink !== 1) {
        throw new Error(`Expected a regular nonlink file: ${path.basename(filename)}`);
    }
    const handle = await fs.open(filename, constants.O_RDONLY | (constants.O_NOFOLLOW ?? 0) |
        (constants.O_NONBLOCK ?? 0));
    try {
        check(); const stat = await handle.stat();
        check();
        if (!stat.isFile() || stat.nlink !== 1 || stat.ino !== before.ino || stat.dev !== before.dev ||
            stat.size > limit) { throw new Error(`Invalid or oversized compiler file: ${path.basename(filename)}`); }
        const buffer = Buffer.alloc(stat.size + 1);
        let length = 0;
        while (length < buffer.length) {
            check(); const read = await handle.read(buffer, length, buffer.length - length, length);
            if (!read.bytesRead) { break; }
            length += read.bytesRead;
        }
        if (length !== stat.size) { throw new Error(`Compiler file changed while reading: ${path.basename(filename)}`); }
        check(); return buffer.subarray(0, length);
    } finally { await handle.close(); }
}
```

Only the final TeX log decides reference convergence: earlier passes normally have
undefined references. Missing glyphs and overfull boxes remain warnings. A bounded
log is mandatory on success; a failed build may not have produced one at all.

```ts : <<publication PDF diagnostics>>=
function errorMessage(error: unknown): string { return error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error); }
function logWarnings(log: string): string[] {
    return [...new Set(log.split(/\r?\n/).filter(line =>
        /Missing character:|Overfull \\[hv]box|(?:LaTeX|Package .*?) Warning:/i.test(line)))];
}

async function failureWarnings(cwd: string): Promise<string[]> {
    try { return logWarnings((await readBoundedFile(path.join(cwd, 'book.log'), maximumLogBytes)).toString('utf8')); }
    catch { return []; }
}
```

Warning headers delimit semantic records. TeX can wrap an unindented line at its
print width; packages also prefix continuation lines with their package name.
Join those lines only inside a warning record, stopping at blank lines or another
recognizable log record. Convergence phrases must begin the warning message, so
ordinary box contents or prose mentioning undefined references cannot fail a build.

```ts : <<publication PDF diagnostics>>=+
function texWarnings(log: string): { source: string; message: string }[] {
    const records: { source: string; message: string }[] = [];
    let current: { source: string; message: string } | undefined;
    for (const line of log.split(/\r?\n/)) {
        const header = /^(LaTeX(?: Font)?|Package [\w.-]+|Class [\w.-]+) Warning(?: \([^)]*\))?:\s*(.*)$/.exec(line);
        if (header) {
            current = { source: header[1], message: header[2] };
            records.push(current);
        } else if (!line.trim() || /^(?:Overfull|Underfull|Missing character:|Latexmk:|!|\[|Output written|Transcript written)/.test(line)) {
            current = undefined;
        } else if (current) {
            current.message += ' ' + line.replace(/^\([\w.-]+\)\s*/, '').trim();
        }
    }
    return records.map(record => ({ ...record, message: record.message.replace(/\s+/g, ' ').trim() }));
}
```

Only final-log warnings determine unresolved references. latexmk exhaustion has
its own anchored diagnostic forms, including its multi-line processing-failure
report. Earlier TeX passes in the console transcript may legitimately request a
rerun and are not inspected as final warnings.

```ts : <<publication PDF diagnostics>>=+
function checkConvergence(log: string, transcript: string): void {
    const unresolved = /^(?:There (?:were|are) undefined (?:references|citations)\b|(?:Reference|Citation)\s+.*\bundefined\b|Label\(s\) may have changed\b|Rerun to get\b|Please (?:\(re\))?run (?:LaTeX|Biber|BibTeX)\b)/i;
    const pending = texWarnings(log).some(record => unresolved.test(record.message) ||
        (record.source === 'Package rerunfilecheck' && /^File\s+.*\bhas changed\b/i.test(record.message)));
    const exhausted = /^Latexmk:[ \t]*(?:Maximum (?:number of )?(?:runs|repeats)\b[^\r\n]*(?:\r?\n[ \t]+[^\r\n]*)?|Failure in processing file[^\r\n]*\r?\n[ \t]+[^\r\n]*\bneeded too many passes\b)/im;
    if (pending || exhausted.test(transcript)) {
        throw new Error('PDF references did not converge; inspect book.log and fix unresolved references or rerun requirements');
    }
}
```

Rail books must also provide a bounded, regular-file stability certificate. A
missing certificate fails only when the entry contains the builder's exact runtime
marker; any present pending/error/malformed certificate fails even without it.
This check happens before PDF bytes can reach the caller's destination transaction.

```ts : <<publication PDF diagnostics>>=+
async function checkRailCertificate(cwd: string, check: () => void): Promise<void> {
    const source = (await readBoundedFile(path.join(cwd, 'book.tex'), maximumPdfBytes, check)).toString('utf8');
    const preamble = source.split('\\begin{document}', 1)[0];
    const expected = /^% literate-publication-rail-v1$/m.test(preamble);
    let certificate: string;
    try {
        certificate = (await readBoundedFile(path.join(cwd, 'book.litrail'), maximumLogBytes, check)).toString('utf8');
    } catch (error) {
        if ((error as NodeJS.ErrnoException).code === 'ENOENT' && !expected) { return; }
        throw new Error(`PDF rail certificate missing or unreadable: ${errorMessage(error)}`);
    }
    if (!/^stable [a-f0-9]{32}\r?\n/.test(certificate)) {
        throw new Error('PDF rail did not converge: book.litrail must certify stable placement');
    }
}
```

latexmk receives only literal arguments. `-norc` disables system, user and project
rc discovery; the literal repeat limit cannot be influenced by an entry basename.
An eight-minute build deadline and five repeats bound runaway reruns while allowing
large books to complete every permitted convergence pass. No `-f` option
can turn a failed pass into apparent success, and shell escape stays disabled.

Final PDF acceptance is presented in [Compiling and publishing a PDF](compiling_publishing_pdf.html).

The factory stages before preflight so retained failures are inspectable. Every
error becomes the exported error type. The success value owns its PDF bytes and
is independent of the deleted stage. Only explicit keepBuild transfers ownership
of that stage to the caller. A final cancellation check occurs after cleanup, so
a signal during deletion cannot turn into a successful compilation.

```ts : <<publication PDF orchestration>>=
async function compileStaged(directory: string, cwd: string, files: Parameters<PdfCompiler>[0],
    runner: PdfProcessRunner, check: () => void): Promise<PdfCompilation> {
    await stageFiles(directory, files, check);
    check();
    const env = await buildEnvironment(directory);
    check();
    const guarded = checkedRunner(runner, check);
    await preflight(guarded, cwd, env);
    check();
    return buildPdf(guarded, cwd, env, check);
}
```

A directory acquired during cancellation is still owned and removed. Failure
warnings are read before deletion, while the lifetime handlers remain installed.
Cancellation takes precedence over a secondary filesystem or tool error.

```ts : <<publication PDF orchestration>>=+
async function compileInStage(files: Parameters<PdfCompiler>[0], entry: string, options: PdfCompileOptions,
    runner: PdfProcessRunner, check: () => void): Promise<PdfCompilation> {
    const directory = await fs.mkdtemp(path.join(tmpdir(), 'literate-pdf-'));
    const cwd = path.join(directory, path.posix.dirname(entry));
    let warnings: string[] = [];
    try {
        let result: PdfCompilation;
        try {
            result = await compileStaged(directory, cwd, files, runner, check);
            warnings = result.warnings;
        } catch (error) {
            warnings = await failureWarnings(cwd);
            throw error;
        } finally {
            if (!options.keepBuild) { await fs.rm(directory, { recursive: true, force: true }); }
        }
        check();
        return options.keepBuild ? { ...result, buildDirectory: directory } : result;
    } catch (error) {
        try { check(); } catch (cancellation) { error = cancellation; }
        throw new PdfCompilationError(errorMessage(error), options.keepBuild ? directory : undefined, warnings);
    }
}
```

Lifetime hooks are acquired before any asynchronous work and disposed only after
the stage has been handled. An optional caller checkpoint carries cancellation
across lazy module loading, before these local signal listeners exist. Synchronous validation errors retain the same public
error type without allocating a build directory.

```ts : <<publication PDF orchestration>>=+
export function createPdfCompiler(runner: PdfProcessRunner = runPdfProcess): PdfCompiler {
    return async (files, entry, options = {}) => {
        const lifetime = compilerLifetime();
        const check = (): void => { lifetime.check(); options.checkCancelled?.(); };
        try {
            check();
            const prepared = prepareFiles(files, entry);
            return await compileInStage(prepared, entry, options, runner, check);
        } catch (error) {
            if (error instanceof PdfCompilationError) { throw error; }
            throw new PdfCompilationError(errorMessage(error));
        } finally { lifetime.dispose(); }
    };
}
export const compilePdf: PdfCompiler = createPdfCompiler();
```
